      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly categorizes the hardware failure type from short_description, which is exactly the axis needed to identify the most common causes and estimate incident-rate reduction. Values in samples are sound: software/MySQL rows correctly get NULL. The rich distribution (Printer, Monitor, Keyboard, etc.) gives strong grouping leverage. Slight uncertainty since only NULL samples are shown, but categorization scheme is well-grounded in a short_description field."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_top_hardware_cause",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A binary flag largely derivable from hardware_failure_cause, so it is partly redundant with the richer column. Still it usefully isolates the top causes for the 'if addressed' counterfactual, so it has some independent leverage. Values are well-grounded and consistent with the cause column (NULL rows -> 0)."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
  "source_groundedness": 0.92,
  "rationale": "Both columns target the query's need to identify and quantify the impact of top hardware failure causes. hardware_failure_cause is the core analytical axis and is well grounded in short_description; is_top_hardware_cause is somewhat derivative but supports the counterfactual framing. No hallucination observed in the samples, and silent/non-hardware rows correctly receive NULL/0."
}

